{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_011GnRCiuPKRAvqtphzfz19G","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Let me analyze the project context carefully and propose tasks.\n\n## Step 1: Analyze Project Context\n\nThis is a specification-driven project that regenerates Unix utilities in Go. The pipeline is: extract specs from C source → specify (PRD + use case) → synthesize (AI code gen) → verify (differential testing).\n\n## Step 2: Summarize Project State\n\n**Problem**: Classic Unix utilities lack formal specifications. Re-implementations diverge on edge cases. This project uses C source as ground truth to create specs, then generates Go implementations and verifies via differential testing.\n\n**Architecture**:\n- `cmd/` - Per-utility Go implementations\n\n\n- `pkg/testutils/` - Differential testing harness\n- `pkg/sys/` - Platform-specific syscall abstractions\n- `pkg/format/` - Shared output formatting\n- `magefiles/` - Build targets\n- `docs/` - Specifications (PRDs, use cases, test suites)\n\n**Current state**: I can see the project has existing implementations for the testing harness, platform abstractions across Darwin and Linux, and output formatting utilities.  The command implementations span cat, du, ls, sponge, version, and wc, with most having corresponding test files. Looking at the release roadmap, the foundational infrastructure batch is spec-complete but code hasn't started yet, with the magefiles and other core components still pending. Looking at the actual source code, I'm noticing a discrepancy—many of these items are marked as \"not started\" but there are already implementations present. The constraint says to trust the source code over documentation, so I need to reconcile this. Checking through the directories, I can see testutils, sys, format, and cat all have existing code, which means the status tracking is out of sync with reality. Looking at what's already implemented, I can see that rel01.3 through rel03.0 are complete with their code and tests, while rel04.0 has the ls implementation but is missing its test file. The real gaps are in rel02.0 through rel02.2, which don't have any code yet since their specs are still pending, and I need to figure out what rel04.1 adds beyond the core ls functionality. I see the constraint now—specs need to be written first, and that's a human task through the make-work/do-work skills, not something I should generate. But for rel04.0 (ls core), the spec is already complete, so I could write tests for the main.go file that exists.\n\nHowever, the constraint limits me to exactly 1 task. Looking at what's actually ready: rel00.0 through rel03.0 all have code and tests already, rel02.0 through rel02.2 are blocked on pending specs, and rel04.0 has the core code but is missing tests. That makes the ls test file the highest priority single task to propose. extended): spec_complete but no code or tests visible\n\nSo the most pressing gap is cmd/ls tests (rel04.0). The implementation exists (main.go) but no test file is shown in source_code.\n\nWait - I need to be careful. The constraint says: \"When source_code contains .go files for a package, that package already exists. Do not propose creating or reimplementing it.\"\n\nSo cmd/ls/main.go already exists. I shouldn't reimplement it. But I CAN propose tests for it since ls_test.go doesn't appear in source_code.\n\nLet me look at the ls implementation more carefully to understand what to test. The current cmd/ls/main.go implements several requirements including single-column output, C locale sorting, hidden file filtering, the -a and -A flags, error handling, and SIGPIPE handling. It also handles the default \".\" argument, multi-argument headers, and distinguishes between file and directory arguments.\n\nI need to map this against the relevant PRD and test suite. The code references prd008-ls, so the test suite would be test-rel-04.0.yaml. Since I can't explore the filesystem directly, I'm working with what's already in the project context to understand what's been implemented and what test coverage is needed. should propose writing ls_test.go to differentially test these.\n\nLooking at the existing test patterns (cat_test.go, du_test.go, wc_test.go, sponge_test.go), they all follow the same structure:\n1. TestMain: build Go binary, find reference binary (gls for ls)\n2. Helper functions (writeTestFile, normalizeProgramName)\n3. Test functions using testutils.RunDiffTests\n\nThe ls implementation covers R1.2, R1.3, R1.4, R3.1, R3.2, R6.1, R6.2, and R7.1, but I need to stay within the 4 sub-requirement limit, so I'll focus on the core features: single-column output, C locale sorting, hidden file filtering, and error handling. That's 8 requirements total. I should narrow this down to at most 4 sub-requirements per task, and since I can only propose one task, I'll focus on the essentials: R1.2, R1.3, R1.4, and R6.2 for basic listing, sorting, hidden file filtering, and error handling—the core differential tests.\n\nLet me reconsider which task would be most impactful. Looking at the release status, rel02.0 through rel02.2 are still pending specs, which requires human work first. But rel04.0 for ls core is spec-complete with existing implementation—it just needs tests. Checking the source code, I notice test files exist for cat, du, sponge, and wc, but there's no ls_test.go file yet, so proposing ls differential tests makes sense.\n\nFor the reference binary, the architecture notes that macOS uses gls and other g-prefixed Homebrew binaries, so gls would be the reference for ls testing. Looking at the requirements the ls command needs to satisfy, I should focus my test on a subset that's manageable—I'll start with R1.2 for single-column output, then add a few related cases to keep the scope reasonable.\n\nNow I'm drafting the actual test task, pulling together the core differential test patterns from the existing test files and specifying what the test should verify about ls's behavior with sorting, hidden files, and error handling. I'm laying out the test requirements for the ls command, covering single-column output, C locale sorting, dotfile handling with different flags, and error handling for nonexistent paths. The design approach follows the existing test pattern from the cat command, building the Go binary in a temp directory and using gls as the reference implementation, with a helper function to normalize error message prefixes so both binaries can be compared directly. AC5 covers hidden-file exclusion and flag behavior, AC6 handles error cases with path normalization, and AC7 requires using the testutils framework for all tests. I'm also double-checking the requirement count against the guidance—I have 4 requirements and 7 acceptance criteria, which fits within the constraints, though I should verify the requirement count isn't too low.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":75060,"cache_read_input_tokens":19289,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":75060},"output_tokens":9,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d8e056ac-dfe8-414f-bfb9-73ece86da4a9","uuid":"cb319e0d-393b-4a46-bf5e-d720c43de173"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_011GnRCiuPKRAvqtphzfz19G","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"## Project State Summary\n\n**Problem**: Classic Unix utilities lack formal specs. This project derives specs from C source, generates Go implementations via AI, and verifies via differential testing against GNU reference binaries.\n\n**Architecture**: `cmd/` (per-utility binaries), `pkg/testutils/` (differential test harness), `pkg/sys/` (platform syscall abstractions), `pkg/format/` (shared formatting), `magefiles/` (build targets), `docs/` (specs).\n\n**Current state**: Source code exists for `pkg/testutils`, `pkg/sys`, `pkg/format`, `cmd/cat` (+tests), `cmd/du` (+tests), `cmd/sponge` (+tests), `cmd/wc` (+tests), `cmd/version`, and `cmd/ls` (implementation only — **no test file**).\n\n**Priority**: Releases 00.0 through 01.3 and 03.0 have both implementation and tests in source code. Releases 02.0–02.2 have pending specs (human work). Release 04.0 (ls core) has spec_complete status and an implementation (`cmd/ls/main.go`) but **no differential test file**. This is the earliest actionable gap.\n\n```yaml\n- index: 0\n  title: \"prd008-ls R1.2, R1.3, R1.4, R6.2 -- ls core differential tests\"\n  dependency: -1\n  description: |\n    deliverable_type: code\n\n    required_reading:\n      - cmd/ls/main.go (ls implementation under test -- read to understand flags and output structure)\n      - cmd/cat/cat_test.go (established differential test pattern to follow)\n      - cmd/du/du_test.go (established differential test pattern with directory trees)\n      - pkg/testutils/difftest.go (DiffTest struct and RunDiffTests API)\n\n    files:\n      - path: cmd/ls/ls_test.go\n        action: create\n        note: Differential tests for ls core listing, sorting, hidden file filtering, and error handling\n\n    requirements:\n      - id: R1\n        text: \"Verify prd008-ls R1.2: ls prints one entry per line in single-column output mode. Run both gls and the Go ls binary on a directory containing multiple files and compare stdout byte-for-byte via testutils.RunDiffTests.\"\n      - id: R2\n        text: \"Verify prd008-ls R1.3: ls sorts entries in C locale byte order (Go sort.Strings). Create a directory with entries whose names sort differently under locale-aware vs byte-order collation (e.g., uppercase vs lowercase) and confirm both binaries produce identical output under LC_ALL=C.\"\n      - id: R3\n        text: \"Verify prd008-ls R1.4: ls excludes entries starting with '.' by default, includes all entries (including . and ..) with -a, and includes dotfiles except . and .. with -A. Run differential tests for each of the three modes.\"\n      - id: R4\n        text: \"Verify prd008-ls R6.2: ls writes a diagnostic to stderr for nonexistent paths, exits with code 1, and continues processing remaining valid arguments. Use a normalizeProgramName function to replace 'gls: ' with 'ls: ' in stderr before comparison.\"\n\n    design_decisions:\n      - id: D1\n        text: \"Follow the TestMain pattern from cmd/cat/cat_test.go and cmd/du/du_test.go: build the Go ls binary into a temp directory via 'go build -o goBinary .', locate the GNU reference binary via exec.LookPath('gls'), and skip all tests with os.Exit(0) if gls is not found on PATH.\"\n      - id: D2\n        text: \"Define a normalizeProgramName helper that replaces 'gls: ' with 'ls: ' in byte slices, following the identical pattern in cmd/cat/cat_test.go (normalizeProgramName replacing 'gcat: ' with 'cat: '). Pass this as a testutils.NormalizeFunc in DiffTest.Normalize for error-handling tests.\"\n      - id: D3\n        text: \"All differential tests rely on pkg/testutils.buildEnv setting LC_ALL=C automatically. Do not set Env overrides in DiffTest unless testing locale-specific behavior. Create test directories with os.MkdirAll and os.WriteFile in t.TempDir() -- do not use fixtures on disk.\"\n\n    acceptance_criteria:\n      - id: AC1\n        text: \"cmd/ls/ls_test.go compiles with no errors when run via 'go build ./cmd/ls/'.\"\n      - id: AC2\n        text: \"TestMain builds the Go ls binary into a temp directory and locates gls via exec.LookPath; all tests are skipped gracefully (os.Exit(0)) when gls is absent.\"\n      - id: AC3\n        text: \"At least one differential test creates a directory with multiple regular files and verifies that both binaries produce identical single-column output (R1.2).\"\n      - id: AC4\n        text: \"At least one differential test creates entries with mixed-case names (e.g., 'Apple', 'banana', 'Cherry') and verifies byte-order sorting matches between binaries (R1.3).\"\n      - id: AC5\n        text: \"At least three differential tests exercise hidden-file filtering: default mode excludes dotfiles, -a includes . and .., -A includes dotfiles but excludes . and .. (R1.4).\"\n      - id: AC6\n        text: \"At least one differential test passes a nonexistent path, expects exit code 1, and applies normalizeProgramName to stderr before comparison (R6.2).\"\n      - id: AC7\n        text: \"At least one differential test passes both a nonexistent path and a valid directory, verifying the valid directory is still listed and exit code is 1 (R6.2 continued processing).\"\n```"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":75060,"cache_read_input_tokens":19289,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":75060},"output_tokens":9,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d8e056ac-dfe8-414f-bfb9-73ece86da4a9","uuid":"7aed3d2d-fb69-4f79-aa79-30e1825610f5"}
